  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Bill Shape as a Diagnostic Feature</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Bill morphology is one of the most useful field characters for separating raven species. Within the ravens, bill shape varies from the moderate curves of the Common Raven to the extreme arched bill of the Thick-billed Raven — the most specialised corvid bill known.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> large, moderately arched, with a culmen curve that becomes more pronounced in older individuals.</li>
      <li><strong>Thick-billed Raven:</strong> massively arched and deep — the bill depth approaches the bill length. Used for prying apart large carcasses; the white ivory-coloured patch at the bill base is diagnostic.</li>
      <li><strong>White-necked Raven:</strong> deeply arched and relatively long; intermediate between Common and Thick-billed.</li>
      <li><strong>Fan-tailed Raven:</strong> comparatively short and straight-edged for a raven; the tail shape is a more reliable diagnostic.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Tail Shape</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Tail shape is immediately visible in flight and is one of the best quick-separation characters between raven species and from crows:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> long, distinctly diamond/wedge-shaped — the central tail feathers project beyond the outer ones.</li>
      <li><strong>Fan-tailed Raven:</strong> short and fan-shaped (rounded), giving the bird a distinctive "compressed" silhouette in flight — the defining field character.</li>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven / Forest Raven:</strong> rounded to slightly graduated tail, shorter wedge than Common Raven.</li>
      <li><strong>Chihuahuan / Brown-necked / White-necked Ravens:</strong> wedge-tailed but with intermediate length between Common Raven and large crows.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat Specialisation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The eight raven species show diverse habitat preferences that largely explain their geographic distributions:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> generalist across habitats — arctic tundra, boreal forest, desert, mountain, agricultural, urban.</li>
      <li><strong>Thick-billed Raven:</strong> highland specialist; restricted to the Ethiopian Plateau above 2,000 m.</li>
      <li><strong>Fan-tailed Raven:</strong> cliff specialist in arid zones; forages in open desert and semi-desert but always close to cliff nesting habitat.</li>
      <li><strong>Brown-necked Raven:</strong> desert and semi-desert specialist across the Sahara, Arabian Peninsula, and into South Asia.</li>
      <li><strong>Chihuahuan Raven:</strong> short-grass prairie and desert scrub specialist; most colonial of the ravens, nesting in loose groups.</li>
      <li><strong>Forest Raven:</strong> temperate forest specialist; more arboreal than other Australian corvids.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

